package "main"

// Two imported packages each export a generic TYPE named `Cursor` that carries a
// parameterized-receiver impl (`impl *Cursor[T] : cit.It[T]`), but with DIFFERENT
// type-parameter constraints: pkg/cga's `Cursor[T any]`, pkg/cgb's
// `Cursor[T lang.Hashable]`.  The checker registers the parameterized-receiver
// impl's receiver-type constraint keyed by the UNQUALIFIED type name `Cursor`, so
// loading both packages collides them: backfillInstantiationMethods re-syncs the
// (cross-package) GenericInstantiations list under the second package's scope, and
// copyPlaceholderMethods' by-unqualified-name placeholder lookup returns the WRONG
// package's `Cursor`, re-instantiating it with the other package's `T` -- so
// pkg/cgb.Cursor's `s @HBag[T]` (HBag needs `T : Hashable`) is re-checked with
// pkg/cga.Cursor's `T any` and false-errors `type argument T does not satisfy
// constraint Hashable` (at cgb.bni:5).  The two imports ALONE reproduce it.
//
// main additionally instantiates one collided generic and calls its
// generic-receiver method (`cur.done()`), so a regression that corrupts METHOD
// backfill (not just the constraint check) is also caught.
//
// Each package compiles cleanly IN ISOLATION; only the combination fails.  This
// is the self-contained minimization of the real defect: any program importing
// two stdx containers whose iteration cursors collide -- e.g.
// pkg/stdx/containers/vec (`Cursor[T any]`) together with
// pkg/stdx/containers/set (`Cursor[T lang.Hashable]`) or /hashmap -- fails to
// compile with the same false error.  A bare same-named generic (no
// parameterized-receiver impl) does NOT collide; the impl registration is the
// trigger.

import "pkg/cga"
import "pkg/cgb"

func main() {
	var cur @cga.Cursor[int] = make(cga.Cursor[int])
	println(cast(int, cur.done())) // 1 (done() returns true)
}
